The vulnerability existed because the `walk_hugetlb_range` function in `mm/pagewalk.c` failed to verify whether a huge page table entry (PTE) was present before processing it. It only checked if the pointer to the PTE slot was non-NULL, not if the entry itself was valid. This allowed a callback function, triggered by a system call like `mincore()`, to be invoked on unmapped memory regions ("holes"), leading to an information leak of uninitialized kernel data.

The patch corrects this flaw by introducing an explicit check, `huge_ptep_present(pte)`, to confirm that the PTE is valid and present before invoking the callback.

**Vulnerable Code:**
```c
	...
		pte = huge_pte_offset(walk->mm, addr & hmask, sz);
		if (pte && walk->hugetlb_entry)
			err = walk->hugetlb_entry(pte, hmask, addr, next, walk);
	...
```

**Patched Code:**
```c
	...
		pte = huge_pte_offset(walk->mm, addr & hmask, sz);
		if (pte && huge_ptep_present(pte) && walk->hugetlb_entry)
			err = walk->hugetlb_entry(pte, hmask, addr, next, walk);
	...
```

